---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import badges from '../../data/aidecl-badges.json';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { LEVELS, describe, type Axis } from '../../lib/aidecl';

const aidecl = 'https://github.com/industrial-mathematics-group/aidecl';
const axes: { key: Axis; letter: string; title: string; question: string; badge: string }[] = [
  { key: 'generation', letter: 'G', title: 'Generation', question: 'How much of it did an AI generate?', badge: 'generation' },
  { key: 'human', letter: 'H', title: 'Human review', question: 'How thoroughly did a human review it?', badge: 'human-review' },
  { key: 'ai', letter: 'A', title: 'AI review', question: 'How thoroughly did an AI review it?', badge: 'ai-review' },
];
const initial = { generation: 2, human: 2, ai: 2 };
const example = describe(initial);
const updated = badges.updated ? new Date(badges.updated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' }) : '';
type Badge = { label: string; message: string; weight: string; shares: { level: string; percent: number }[] };
const kinds = [
  { key: 'files', title: 'Files', note: 'declared in AIDECL.toml' },
  { key: 'changes', title: 'Pull requests', note: 'declared in the merged pull requests' },
] as const;
---

<Layout title="AI declarations" description="How we declare the use of AI in our code, and aidecl, the tool that checks it.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>AI declarations</h1>
      <p>We use AI tools in our work, and every contribution says how it was created and how it was checked. A declaration such as <strong>G2 · H2 · A2</strong> reads: generated by AI, reviewed line by line by a human, and reviewed by a new AI session.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Three scales</h2>
      <div class="scales">
        {axes.map((axis) => (
          <div class={`scale scale-${axis.badge}`}>
            <h3>{axis.letter}: {axis.title}</h3>
            <p class="scale-question">{axis.question}</p>
            <ol>
              {LEVELS[axis.key].map((l) => (
                <li><strong>{l.level} {l.name}.</strong> {l.meaning}</li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          </div>
        ))}
        <div class="prose scale-rules">
          <p>Two rules connect the scales. AI-written content must at least be checked by the session that wrote it, so G1 to G3 need A1 or higher. Human-written content has no such session, so G0 is never A1: an AI review of it counts as A2 or A3. A human is always responsible, whatever wrote the code, and reviews at A2 or A3 are committed to the repository.</p>
          <p><a href={site.aiPolicy}>The full AI policy</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build a declaration</h2>
      <div>
        <form class="builder" data-builder>
          {axes.map((axis) => (
            <fieldset class={`scale-${axis.badge}`}>
              <legend>{axis.title}</legend>
              {LEVELS[axis.key].map((l, i) => (
                <label title={l.meaning}>
                  <input type="radio" name={axis.key} value={i} checked={initial[axis.key] === i} />
                  <span>{l.level}</span> {l.name}
                </label>
              ))}
            </fieldset>
          ))}
        </form>
        <div class="builder-result" aria-live="polite">
          <p class="builder-label" data-builder-label>{example.label}</p>
          <p data-builder-sentence>{example.sentence}</p>
          <ul class="builder-problems" data-builder-problems hidden></ul>
          <p class="builder-caption">In <code>AIDECL.toml</code>, for the files in <code>src/</code>:</p>
          <pre><code data-builder-toml>{example.toml}</code></pre>
          <p class="builder-caption">Or written by the tool:</p>
          <pre><code data-builder-command>{example.command}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Two places to declare</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>Pull requests</strong> declare each change. The pull request template asks a few questions, a check derives the levels from the ticked boxes, labels the pull request and keeps the declaration in the history of the main branch. This suits code that is developed through pull requests.</p>
        <p><strong>Files</strong> declare the current state of a repository in an <code>AIDECL.toml</code>, which works like <a href="https://reuse.software">REUSE</a> does for licences: paths with their levels, the general default first and the exceptions after it. Source files can also carry the declaration in a comment in their first lines. This suits results, data, notebooks and papers, and repositories that are published in a single commit.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Our repositories</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="prose">The repositories publish badges that sum up their declarations: the share of lines (or files) at each level. These are the current ones{updated && <>, as of {updated}</>}.</p>
        <div class="badge-list">
          {badges.repositories.filter((r) => r.files || r.changes).map((repo) => (
            <div class="badge-repo">
              <h3><a href={repo.url}>{repo.repo}</a></h3>
              {kinds.map((kind) => {
                const set = repo[kind.key] as Record<string, Badge> | undefined;
                return set && (
                  <div class="badge-kind">
                    <p class="badge-kind-title">{kind.title} <span>{kind.note}{set.generation.weight === 'lines' ? ', weighted by lines' : ''}</span></p>
                    {axes.map((axis) => {
                      const badge = set[axis.badge];
                      return (
                        <div class={`decl-badge scale-${axis.badge}`}>
                          <span class="decl-badge-label">{axis.title}</span>
                          <span class="decl-badge-bar" role="img" aria-label={`${axis.title}: ${badge.message}`}>
                            {badge.shares.map((s) => (
                              <span class={`share share-${s.level.at(-1)}${s.level === 'undeclared' ? ' share-undeclared' : ''}`} style={`flex-grow: ${s.percent}`} title={`${s.level} ${s.percent}%`}>
                                {s.percent >= 14 ? `${s.level} ${s.percent}%` : s.percent >= 6 ? s.level : ''}
                              </span>
                            ))}
                          </span>
                        </div>
                      );
                    })}
                  </div>
                );
              })}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="figure-note">Fainter shades are lower levels. The badges are made for the READMEs of the repositories; this site copies them at every deployment, so your browser loads nothing from GitHub to show them here.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The tool: aidecl</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><a href={aidecl}>aidecl</a> checks file declarations, like <code>reuse lint</code> checks licences. It needs Python 3.11 or newer and nothing else:</p>
        <pre><code>pipx install "git+https://github.com/industrial-mathematics-group/aidecl"

# a default for every file
aidecl init -g G0 -H H2 -a A0 --reviewed-by "Jane Doe"

# the exceptions, by file, folder or glob
aidecl annotate results/ -g G2 -H H1 -a A1 \
    --assisted-by "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)" \
    --ai-reviewed-by "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), same session" \
    --reviewed-by "Jane Doe"

# is every file declared?
aidecl lint</code></pre>
        <p><code>annotate</code> refuses declarations that break the rules above, <code>lint</code> fails while any file lacks a valid declaration, and <code>badge</code> writes the badges shown on this page. A repository can raise the minimum levels in a <code>[policy]</code> table, for example a line-by-line human review for everything an agent wrote.</p>
        <p>Our repositories run it in a ready-made workflow on every push and pull request, which also publishes the badges. <code>aidecl migrate</code> converts declarations from the earlier versions of the policy.</p>
        <p><a href={aidecl}>aidecl on GitHub</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import { describe } from '../../lib/aidecl';

  const form = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-builder]');
  const field = (name: string) => document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-builder-${name}]`)!;
  if (form) {
    const update = () => {
      const data = new FormData(form);
      const d = describe({ generation: Number(data.get('generation')), human: Number(data.get('human')), ai: Number(data.get('ai')) });
      field('label').textContent = d.label;
      field('sentence').textContent = d.sentence;
      field('toml').textContent = d.toml;
      field('command').textContent = d.command;
      const list = field('problems');
      list.replaceChildren(...d.problems.map((text) => Object.assign(document.createElement('li'), { textContent: text })));
      list.hidden = d.problems.length === 0;
      field('label').classList.toggle('is-invalid', d.problems.length > 0);
    };
    form.addEventListener('change', update);
    update();
  }
</script>
